        # ---------------------------------------------------------------- the idea
        dict(kind='idea', section='The idea', title='A general assistant knows what it read in training, and has never seen your handbook',
             visual=dict(kind='l08_knows'),
             lead='Training gave it the public world. Your files were never in it.',
             points=['Asked ten handbook questions with no handbook, both runs said “not stated” to all ten.', 'A careful tool declines.', 'A less careful one guesses.'],
             notes='Start with what a general assistant knows. As Session 5 showed, it learned to write from a huge library of public text, up to a cut-off date. On the left: books, web pages, general knowledge. On the right: what it has never seen. Your staff handbook, this month’s price list, your contracts. Those are private, so they were never in its training. In our recorded runs, when we asked ten questions about a fictional staff handbook and gave no handbook, both runs answered not stated to all ten. That is the careful outcome. A less careful tool guesses, and Session 5 showed how sure a guess can sound. So we must hand it the pages.'),
        dict(kind='wide', title='An assistant on your documents finds the right pages, answers from them, and shows the source',
             visual=dict(kind='l08_openbook'),
             caption='Vendors call the three steps together retrieval-augmented generation, or RAG.',
             notes='A good student in an open-book test does three things. First, finds the right pages in the book. Second, answers from those pages, and from nothing else. Third, quotes the page, so anyone can check. An assistant on your documents does the same. Step one is called retrieval: it searches your documents by meaning, as in Session 4, and keeps the closest few pages. Step two is generation: it writes the answer from those pages. Step three is the citation: the clause or page it used. Together the industry calls this retrieval-augmented generation, RAG for short. When a vendor says RAG, this picture is what they mean. Each step comes next on a recorded run.'),
        dict(kind='idea', title='Step one ranks every page by how close its meaning is to the question',
             visual=dict(kind='l08_find'),
             lead='The closest pages go to the assistant. The rest stay on the shelf.',
             points=['Thirty pieces, one per clause.', 'Clause 3.2 came top, well ahead.', '3.1 and 3.5 share words and hold no answer.'],
             notes='Step one, on a recorded run. The handbook of Nile Valley Foods, our fictional company, was cut into thirty pieces, one per numbered clause. The question: who must approve an expense claim of UGX 1,500,000? The search compared the meaning of the question with every clause, as in Session 4, and ranked them. The longer the bar, the closer the meaning. Clause 3.2, on approval limits, came top, well ahead of the next. Then 3.1 on receipts and 3.5 on when claims are paid, which share words but hold no answer. The three closest, in gold, go to the assistant. The others stay on the shelf.'),
        dict(kind='idea', title='Step two answers only from those pages, and names the clause it used',
             visual=dict(kind='l08_answer'),
             lead='One instruction does the work: answer only from these pages, or say “not stated”.',
             points=['It worked out the band itself.', 'Anyone can open clause 3.2 and check.', 'Both runs gave the same answer.'],
             notes='With three clauses in hand, the assistant writes. The instruction is short: answer only from these pages, name the clause, and if the pages do not say, write not stated. On the left are the three clauses it received, shortened. On the right is the recorded answer: the Finance Manager, because one and a half million falls in the band from 200,001 to two million shillings, clause 3.2. Two things matter for a leader. It worked out the band from the page; nobody wrote that answer down in advance. And it named the clause, so any member of staff can open the handbook and check. Both runs gave the same answer. Everything depends on the pages being right.'),
        dict(kind='idea', title='Messy documents give messy answers',
             visual=dict(kind='l08_messy'),
             lead='The assistant answers from whatever it finds, right or wrong.',
             points=['These four examples are illustrative.', 'Farmer.Chat left one question in four unanswered.', 'Its library had gaps.'],
             notes='The assistant answers from whatever it finds, so the state of your documents becomes the quality of its answers. Four problems appear in most organisations, and these examples are illustrative. Two versions disagree: version 3 says two days of notice, version 4 says fourteen. Which one did it find? Out of date: last year’s price list still sits in the folder. Unreadable: a photocopied scan or a table the machine cannot follow. And never written down: the rule lives in one manager’s head, so no page holds it. Digital Green’s farm chatbot in Kenya left about one question in four unanswered, mostly because its library had gaps. Even tidy documents can be cut badly.'),
        dict(kind='idea', title='Where you cut the pages decides what can be found: 8 of 8 by clause, 5 of 8 in short slices',
             visual=dict(kind='l08_cutting'),
             lead='Cut along the document’s own sections, and check.',
             points=['The industry calls the pieces chunks.', 'A split clause hid three answers.', 'Keep headings with their text.'],
             notes='Before the search, the documents are cut into pieces, which the industry calls chunks. Where you cut matters, and this recorded test used the same handbook and the eight questions it can answer. Cut at each numbered clause: the top piece held the whole answer for 8 of 8. Cut every 150 characters, about 25 words: only 5 of 8, because a clause was split and its answer landed in the next piece. Cut every 300 characters: 8 of 8 again. Cut along your document’s own sections, keep headings with their text, and test it on questions with known answers. When the document is short, you may not need to cut at all.'),
        dict(kind='idea', title='A short document can be pasted in whole',
             visual=dict(kind='l08_paste'),
             lead='If it fits, paste it all. If it does not, find the pages first.',
             points=['The handbook: 829 words, about 3 pages.', 'Pasted whole: 10 of 10 in both runs.', 'A library of thousands of pages needs retrieval.'],
             notes='Not every case needs a search. An assistant can read a certain amount of text at once, your question and pages included, and the industry calls that the context window. Today’s assistants accept long documents. Our handbook is 829 words, about three pages, so we pasted the whole of it, and both recorded runs got 10 of 10. That is the easiest route, and often the right first one. A whole policy library is different: thousands of pages will not fit, and each question would send everything again, which is slower and usually dearer. There, find the right pages first. One more route remains, the one vendors like to sell: customising the model.'),
        dict(kind='wide', title='Customising a model teaches it a style, and it still invents the facts',
             visual=dict(kind='l08_style'),
             caption='Trained on 48 replies in 42 seconds: it learned the sign-off, and still made up a Monday delivery.',
             notes='Customising means fine-tuning, which you met in Session 6: training an existing model further on your own examples, as Sunbird did. A cheaper way that changes only a small share of the model is called LoRA, short for low-rank adaptation, and you only need the name. In this recorded run a small, old open model learned from 48 invented customer replies written in one house style, in 42 seconds on a laptop. Before, it rambled. After, all six new replies ended with the house sign-off, against none before. The middle reply says we will arrive on Monday, and no date was ever given to it. It learned how we write, and it still invented what happened.'),
        dict(kind='idea', title='Prompt first, then retrieve, and customise only for style',
             visual=dict(kind='l08_climb'),
             lead='Start on the lowest step that does the job.',
             points=['Each step up costs more and changes more slowly.', 'Test at every step on known answers.', 'This picture is today’s decision sheet.'],
             notes='Put the three routes together and you have a climb. Step one, prompt: a good brief from Session 7, with short documents pasted in. It is cheap, quick and easy to change, so start there. Step two, retrieve: when the documents are many, change often, or each answer must show its source. Step three, customise: only when you need a fixed style or format at large volume, and never to teach facts, because, as we just saw, it still invents them. Climb a step only when the one below falls short, and test at each step on questions whose answers you know. Your decision sheet today is this picture.'),
        dict(kind='idea', title='Assistants on your documents are good at repeat questions, and bad where the pages are thin',
             visual=dict(kind='l08_good_bad'),
             lead='Use it where the answer is written down and asked for often.',
             points=['Change the page and the next answer changes.', 'No page behind it: no answer to trust.'],
             notes='Assistants on your documents are good at the same policy questions asked all day, at long scattered documents nobody reads end to end, at pointing to the page so staff can check, and at staying current, because you change the page and the next answer changes with it. They are bad where the pages are thin: gaps and contradictions, questions that need a judgement the documents do not make, numbers buried in scans and tables, and any answer with no page behind it. So the best first uses are written rules asked about often, and several organisations near your own industries have found them.'),
